      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.217,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "POLYNOMIALS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.872,
            0.105,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "21"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.139,
            0.902,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Example 4 : Find a quadratic polynomial, the sum and product of whose zeroes are –3 and 2, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.183,
            0.898,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "Solution : Let the quadratic polynomial be $a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c ,$ and its zeroes be α and $\\beta .$ We have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.31,
            0.235,
            0.502,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "\\alpha + \\beta = - 3 = { \\frac { - b } { a } } ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.306,
            0.141,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "and"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.337,
            0.297,
            0.472,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "\\alpha \\beta = 2 = { \\frac { c } { a } } \\cdot",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.343,
            0.385,
            0.361
          ],
          "content": "If $a = 1$ , then $b = 3$ and $c = 2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.367,
            0.805,
            0.387
          ],
          "content": "So, one quadratic polynomial which fits the given conditions is $x ^ { 2 } + 3 x \\pm 2 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.393,
            0.9,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": "You can check that any other quadratic polynomial that fits these conditions will be of the form $k ( x ^ { 2 } + 3 x + 2 )$ , where k is real."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.438,
            0.9,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": "Let us now look at cubic polynomials. Do you think a similar relation holds between the zeroes of a cubic polynomial and its coefficients?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.481,
            0.564,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider $p(x) = 2x^3 - 5x^2 - 14x + 8.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.515,
            0.899,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": "You can check that $p(x)=0  for  x=4,-2,\\frac{1}{2};$ Since $p ( x )$ can have atmost three zeroes, these are the zeores of $2x^{3}-5x^{2}-14x+8$ Now,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.582,
            0.891,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "sum of the zeroes $4+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{-(-5)}{2}=\\frac{-(Coefficient of x^{2})}{Coefficient of x^{3}}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.639,
            0.838,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": "product of the zeroes $4 \\times (-2) \\times \\frac{1}{2} = -4 = \\frac{-8}{2} = \\frac{-  constant term }{  Coefficient of   x^3}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.694,
            0.901,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "However, there is one more relationship here. Consider the sum of the products of the zeroes taken two at a time. We have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.744,
            0.77,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": "\\left\\{ 4 \\times ( - 2 ) \\right\\} + \\left\\{ ( - 2 ) \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\right\\} + \\left\\{ \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\right\\} = - 8 - 1 + 2 = - 7 = \\frac{- 14}{2} = \\frac{ Coefficient of   x}{ Coefficient of   x^{3}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.857,
            0.9,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "In general, it can be proved that if α, β, γ are the zeroes of the cubic polynomial $a x ^ { 3 } + b x ^ { 2 } + c x + d ,$ then"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.564,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
